{"cuisine/chilean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chilean-cuisine","n":"Chilean cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Chile","intro":[["Chilean cuisine stems mainly from the combination of traditional Spanish cuisine, Chilean Mapuche culture and local ingredients, with later important influences from other European cuisines, particularly from Germany, the United Kingdom and France."],["The food tradition and recipes in Chile are notable for the variety of flavours and ingredients, with the country's diverse geography and climate hosting a wide range of agricultural produce, fruits and vegetables. The long coastline and the peoples' relationship with the Pacific Ocean add a notable array of seafood to Chilean cuisine, with the country's waters home to unique species of fish, molluscs, crustaceans and algae, thanks to the oxygen-rich water carried in by the Humboldt Current. Chile is also one of the world's largest producers of wine and many Chilean recipes are enhanced and accompanied by local wines. The confection dulce de leche was invented in Chile and is one of the country's most notable contributions to world cuisine."],["Chilean cuisine shares some similarities with Mediterranean cuisine, as the Matorral region, stretching from 32° to 37° south, is one of the world's five Mediterranean climate zones."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chilean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Curanto","dish/curanto"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Mi Diario de Cocina: Chilean Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"midiariodecocina.com","note":"Bilingual blog with a large collection of traditional Chilean recipes.","url":"https://www.midiariodecocina.com/en/chilean-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Pilar's Chilean Food & Garden","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chileanfoodandgarden.com","note":"Chilean home cooking blog by Pilar Hernández.","url":"https://www.chileanfoodandgarden.com/?p=37406","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Chilean Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Pilar Hernández and Eileen Smith","note":"Traditional Chilean recipes from the author of the Chilean Food & Garden blog.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Capsicum chinense","ingredient/capsicum-chinense"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Shellfish","ingredient/shellfish"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"More from Chile","count":2,"links":[["Mapuche foodways","cuisine/mapuche-foodways"],["Pascuense (Easter Island) cuisine","cuisine/pascuense-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8b/Chilean_food.jpg/500px-Chilean_food.jpg","w":500,"h":345,"by":"Photocapy","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilean_food.jpg"}},"dish/anzac-biscuit":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/anzac-biscuit","n":"Anzac biscuit","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#55b8ad","intro":[["The Anzac biscuit is a sweet biscuit, popular in Australia and New Zealand, made using rolled oats, flour, sugar, butter (or margarine), golden syrup, baking soda, boiling water and optionally desiccated coconut. Anzac biscuits have long been associated with the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps (ANZAC), established in World War I. It is thought that these biscuits were sent by wives and women's groups to soldiers abroad because they kept well in transport."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-anzac-biscuit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anzac_biscuit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[[