h/stornoway-black-pudding"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Domestic duck","ingredient/domestic-duck"],["Domestic goose","ingredient/domestic-goose"],["Liver","ingredient/liver"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Brine","ingredient/brine"],["Kidney","ingredient/kidney"],["Kidneys as food","ingredient/kidneys-as-food"],["Pearl barley","ingredient/pearl-barley"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Avena","ingredient/avena"],["Blood as food","ingredient/blood-as-food"],["Livestock","ingredient/livestock"],["Oatmeal","ingredient/oatmeal"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2f/Pig%27s_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg/500px-Pig%27s_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg","w":500,"h":403,"by":"FotoosVanRobin from Netherlands","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pig's_liver_with_sauteed_onion.jpg"}},"dish/garlic-bread":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/garlic-bread","n":"Garlic bread","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian-American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Garlic bread (also called garlic toast) is a dish consisting of crusty bread slices topped with garlic and butter, and additional herbs such as oregano, chives or parsley. Some versions include cheese. It is then either grilled until toasted or baked in a conventional or bread oven."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-garlic-bread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garlic_bread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Baguette","ingredient/baguette"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"]]},{"h":"More from Italian-American cuisine","links":[["Chicken Francese","dish/chicken-francese"],["Fettuccine Alfredo","dish/fettuccine-alfredo"],["Italian beef","dish/italian-beef"],["Italian tomato pie","dish/italian-tomato-pie"],["Rollatini","dish/rollatini"],["Spaghetti and meatballs","dish/spaghetti-and-meatballs"],["Stromboli (food)","dish/stromboli-food"],["Tetrazzini","dish/tetrazzini"],["Vodka sauce","dish/vodka-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/59/Garlicbread.jpg/500px-Garlicbread.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garlicbread.jpg"}},"dish/japanese-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/japanese-restaurant","n":"Japanese restaurant","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Japanese cuisine encompasses the regional and traditional foods of Japan, which have developed through centuries of political, economic, and social changes. The traditional cuisine of Japan is based on rice with miso soup and other dishes with an emphasis on seasonal ingredients. Side dishes often consist of fish, pickled vegetables, tamagoyaki, and vegetables cooked in broth. Common seafood is often grilled, but it is also sometimes served raw as sashimi or as sushi. Seafood and vegetables are also deep-fried in a light batter, as tempura. Apart from rice, a staple includes noodles, such as soba and udon. Japan also has many simmered dishes, such as fish products in broth called oden, or beef in sukiyaki and nikujaga."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-japanese-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_restaurant"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kōji (food)","dish/koji-food"],["Kusa mochi","dish/kusa-mochi"],["Kushikatsu","dish/kushikatsu"],["List of Japanese restaurants","dish/list-of-japanese-restaurants"],["Makizushi","dish/makizushi"],["Miso","dish/miso"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Monjayaki","dish/monjayaki"],["Nikujaga","dish/nikujaga"],["Nimono","dish/nimono"],["Nori","dish/nori"],["Oden","dish/oden"],["Onigiri","dish/onigiri"],["Osechi","dish/osechi"],["Oyakodon","dish/oyakodon"],["Rainbow roll","dish/rainbow-roll"],["Ramen","dish/ramen"],["Ryōtei","dish/ryotei"],["Sashimi","dish/sashimi"],["Sencha","dish/sencha"],["Shizuoka oden","dish/shizuoka-oden"],["Sichuan pepper","dish/sichuan-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2e/Sumo_Japanese_Restaurant.jpg/500px-Sumo_Japanese_Restaurant.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Chongkian","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumo_Japanese_Restaurant.jpg"}},"ingredient/self-rising-flour":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/self-rising-flour","n":"Self-rising flour","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Flour is a powder used to make many different foods, including baked goods, as well as thickening dishes. It is made by grinding grains, beans, nuts, seeds, roots, or vegetables using a mill."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-self-rising-flour"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-rising_flour"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine",[["Lahoh","dish/lahoh"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Sorghum flour","ingredient/sorghum-flour"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]}]},"dish/mojo-sauce":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mojo-sauce","n":"Mojo (sauce)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Mojo is the name, or abbreviated name, of several types of sauces, varying in spiciness, consisting primarily of olive oil, local pepper varieties (called pimienta in Spain), garlic, paprika (called pimentón in Spain), cumin or coriander, and other spices. Mojo originated in the Canary Islands, where the main varieties are green mojo (mojo verde), red mojo (mojo rojo), and spicy red mojo (mojo picón). Other countries have recipes similar to mojo, where acidic ingredients such as vinegar, lemon, orange, or lime juice may be used."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mojo-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojo_(sauce)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Canarian cuisine","cuisine/canarian-cuisine"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Carum","ingredient/carum"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Canarian cuisine","links":[["Almogrote","dish/almogrote"],["Papas arrugadas","dish/papas-arrugadas"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7f/Visita_a_Har%C3%ADa_%2809-2021%29_04.jpg/500px-Visita_a_Har%C3%ADa_%2809-2021%29_04.jpg","w":500,"h":889,"by":"MiguelAlanCS","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visita_a_Har%C3%ADa_(09-2021)_04.jpg"}},"ingredient/blue-mussel":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/blue-mussel","n":"Blue mussel","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The blue mussel (Mytilus edulis), also known as the common mussel, is a medium-sized edible marine bivalve mollusc in the family Mytilidae, the only extant family in the order Mytilida, known as \"true mussels\". Blue mussels are subject to commercial use and intensive aquaculture. A species with a large range, the blue mussel leaves empty shells that are commonly found on beaches around the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-blue-mussel"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_mussel"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine",[["Moules-frites","dish/moules-frites"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Fries","ingredient/fries"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a9/Miesmuscheln-2.jpg/500px-Miesmuscheln-2.jpg","w":500,"h":939,"by":"Rainer Zenz","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miesmuscheln-2.jpg"}},"dish/papanasi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/papanasi","n":"Papanași","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Papanași is a Romanian dessert made from fresh-cheese (brânza proaspătă or urdă) dough. It is usually served topped with cream (smântână) and fruit confit. It is a traditional dessert in  Romania and Moldova."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-papanasi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papana%C8%99i"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Moldovan cuisine","cuisine/moldovan-cuisine"],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Notes from Irina's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"irinageorgescu.substack.com","note":"Newsletter of Romanian recipes and food stories from cookbook author Irina Georgescu.","url":"https://irinageorgescu.substack.com/about","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Moldovan cuisine","links":[["Blini","dish/blini"],["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Ostropel","dish/ostropel"],["Sarmale","dish/sarmale"],["Scorcolga","dish/scorcolga"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Papanasi_cu_cirese.jpg/500px-Papanasi_cu_cirese.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Nicubunu","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papanasi_cu_cirese.jpg"}},"dish/rugao-ham":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/rugao-ham","n":"Rugao ham","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Rugao ham is a  dry-cured ham that originated in Jiangsu province, China. It dates to the Qing dynasty, and was first prepared circa 1851. Rugao ham is produced in a variety of flavors, colors and weights. The local breed of Jiangquhai pigs are typically used for the ham. In contemporary times, it is produced in Rugao, Jiangsu province, which the ham is named after. It is a well-known ham in China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-rugao-ham"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugao_ham"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao","dish/cha-siu-bao"],["Chinese restaurant","dish/chinese-restaurant"],["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"],["Dapanji","dish/dapanji"],["Dong'an chicken","dish/dong-an-chicken"],["Dongpo pork","dish/dongpo-pork"],["Donkey burger","dish/donkey-burger"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Drunken shrimp","dish/drunken-shrimp"],["Edible bird's nest","dish/edible-bird-s-nest"],["Fish sauce","dish/fish-sauce"],["Fuqi feipian","dish/fuqi-feipian"],["Gaifan","dish/gaifan"],["Goubuli","dish/goubuli"]